    "question": "What is the primary distinction between linear and non‑linear data structures?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Linear structures have a single level of hierarchy, while non‑linear structures have multiple levels.",
      "B": "Linear structures store elements in contiguous memory, whereas non‑linear structures always use linked memory.",
      "C": "Linear structures support only sequential access, while non‑linear structures support random access.",
      "D": "Linear structures can be implemented without pointers, but non‑linear structures cannot."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "Linear data structures (e.g., arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues) arrange elements in a single sequence; non‑linear structures (e.g., trees, graphs) organize elements in a hierarchy with multiple branches.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which statement best describes the difference between static and dynamic data structures?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Static structures have a size fixed at compile time, while dynamic structures can change size during execution.",
      "B": "Static structures are always stored on the heap, whereas dynamic structures reside on the stack.",
      "C": "Static structures support only sequential access, while dynamic structures support only random access.",
      "D": "Static structures require pointers for each element, while dynamic structures never use pointers."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "Static data structures (e.g., fixed‑size arrays) have a predetermined size, whereas dynamic structures (e.g., linked lists, dynamic arrays) can grow or shrink at runtime.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which operation cannot be guaranteed to run in O(1) time on a singly linked list?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Insert a new node at the head of the list",
      "B": "Delete the first node",
      "C": "Access the last node",
      "D": "Traverse the list to count its elements"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "In a singly linked list, reaching the last node requires traversing all preceding nodes, giving O(n) time; the other operations can be done in constant time with a reference to the head.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following statements about arrays is true?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Elements are stored in non‑contiguous memory locations, allowing flexible resizing.",
      "B": "The size of an array must be known at compile time and cannot be changed at runtime.",
      "C": "Random access to any element takes O(1) time because of direct indexing.",
      "D": "Insertion at an arbitrary position always takes O(1) time."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Arrays allocate contiguous memory, enabling direct index calculation; therefore, accessing any element by index is O(1). The other statements are false.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following is a primitive data type commonly used as a building block for more complex data structures?",
    "options": {
      "A": "int",
      "B": "struct",
      "C": "class",
      "D": "pointer"
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "Primitive types such as `int` (or `char`, `float`) are the basic units from which arrays, nodes, and other structures are constructed; the others are composite or derived types.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  }
